4. Temperature : It is the property of an object that determines direction in which energy will flow when that object is in contact with other object.
In scientific measurements temperature (T) is measured either on the celsius scale (°C) or the Kelvin scale (K). (Note that degree sign is not used in Kelvin unit).
SI Unit of Temperature of a gas is kelvin (K). The celsius and kelvin scales are related by the expression
T(K) = t^0 C + 273.15
5. Density: It is the mass per unit volume.
d = \frac{m}{V}
 SI Unit of density is kg m&lt;sup&gt;-3&lt;/sup&gt;.
In the case of gases, relative density is measured with respect to hydrogen gas and is called vapour density.
\therefore \text{ Vapour density} = \frac{\text{molar mass}}{2}
6. Diffusion: In case of gases, Rate of diffusion of two or more gases is measured.
Rate of = diffusion \frac{\text{Volume of a gas diffused}}{\text{time required for diffusion}}
\therefore SI Unit for Rate of diffusion is dm&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; s&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; or cm&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; s&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;
